# PHA-2194: logrotate stanza for the anthropic-proxy JSONL access log.
#
# Install on the host (NOT inside the container) — alpine images don't ship
# logrotate. Place at /etc/logrotate.d/anthropic-proxy on the Unraid host,
# then `logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/anthropic-proxy` to dry-run.
#
# The container's VOLUME /var/log/anthropic-proxy is bind-mounted to wherever
# the Unraid container config maps it; this stanza assumes the canonical
# /var/log/anthropic-proxy path on the host. Adjust the path if your bind
# mount differs.
#
# Rotation policy:
#   - daily: small enough to make a single-rotation blowup cheap
#   - rotate 7: keep a week of history; adjust up if you need more
#   - compress: gzipped logs are ~5% of raw size at this throughput
#   - copytruncate: tells logrotate to rename + reopen rather than asking
#     the writer to close/reopen its fd. The proxy opens LOG_FILE with
#     `flags: 'a'` once at startup and never reopens — without
#     copytruncate, rotation will silently keep writing into the rotated
#     file until restart.
#
# Why not dateext: simpler rotate-N scheme is fine for a single-host
# log that nobody parses by filename; dateext is a footgun if you ever
# run multiple instances writing to the same path.

/var/log/anthropic-proxy/access.jsonl {
    daily
    rotate 7
    missingok
    notifempty
    compress
    delaycompress
    copytruncate
    create 0640 node node
}